   Inst Architects; Hong Kong Institut Engineers; Hong Kong Inst Surveyors;
   HKSTS; IUE; JSCE; RICS; Urban Planning Soc China; China Civil Engn Soc;
   Hong Kong Inst Util Specialists},
Abstract = {Ecological factor has not always been the main factor to be concerned
   with when planning a city, thus a city has always been forgotten to be
   regarded as an ecosystem. If the city is treated as an ecosystem, there
   is a need to conserve adequate green space to fulfill the ecosystem
   services. Although the Malaysian statutory planning system through its
   development plan provides allocation of open space, the contribution of
   these open spaces towards urban ecosystem is still questionable. It
   should be noted that the term open space is given statutory definition
   by the Malaysian planning statute. This paper presents a study on the
   policy direction and the guidelines outlined by the development plans
   which primarily form the basis for determining planning application. For
   this purpose, the Johor Bahru District was selected and guidelines on
   the provision of open space in the relevant development plans were
   studied using the method of content analysis. The provision of open
   spaces is consequently assessed based on landscape ecological
   principles. The paper concludes with recommendation on the need to
   integrate these principles with the conventional land use planning to
   enhance the quality of green space in the quest for sustainable
   development.},
