   Survey, Biol Resources Div},
Abstract = {Landscapes protect biological diversity and provide unique opportunities
   for human-nature interactions. Too often, these desirable settings
   suffer from extremely high visitation. Given the complexity of social,
   environmental and economic interactions, resource managers need tools
   that provide insights into the cause and effect relationships between
   management actions and social and environmental outcomes.
   Object-oriented technology, along with spatial gee-referenced temporal
   data, provides new opportunities for developing, testing and improving
   simulation models. This paper describes a case study in Sedona, Arizona,
   that utilizes prototype software package the Recreation Behaviour
   Simulator (RBSim), to dynamically simulate the behavior of human
   recreationists' in high-use natural environments.},
