Abstract = {An integrated approach to stormwater management is being increasingly
   promoted as a fundamental method of developing the sustainable
   management water in the urban environment. It is necessary to develop
   new sustainable solutions in urban planning, whose purpose is to
   mitigate the negative impact of the traditional drainage systems and to
   preserve the hydrological cycle of a site bringing it back before the
   urban development. This study considers the concept of Blue-Green
   Corridors (BGC) and how the incorporation of these into spatial planning
   of the urban environment can be used to prevent the generation of
   surface water and mitigate the effects of surface water and fluvial
   flooding. In order to assess the feasibility of BGC implementation, as a
   network of blue-green components across a catchment that links green
   spaces and blue elements with the river corridor, a case study site
   (Liguori Catchment - LC, in Cosenza) has been used. Impervious building
   roofs have been replaced with green roofs; whereas a parking lot has
   been substituted with a green zone. These latters have been connected to
   the blue elements of the system by streets made of permeable pavements.
   A topography analysis on DTM of the area (derived from LiDAR) has been
   conducted (using ESRI ArcGIS). Hydrologic modelling (using SWMM and
   historical rainfall data) demonstrated that the implementation of the
   proposed plan, peak runoff rates and volume can be reduced.},
