   Transfer, Hefei, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY 24-25, 2012},
Organization = {Hefei Univ; Univ Rostock; State Univ N Carolina; Hefei Assoc Sci \&
   Technol; Anhui Environm Sci Soc},
Abstract = {Urban dust samples from fifty-two sampling sites on the impervious land
   surfaces in Hefei, China were collected and analyzed. The results showed
   that the mean concentrations of copper (Cu), zinc (Zn), lead (Pb),
   chromium (Cr) and cadmium (Cd) in urban dusts from Hefei were up to
   67.50, 2332.96, 132.15, 96.88 and 4.53mg/kg, respectively, all of which
   are much higher than the soil background values of Anhui Province. Based
   on the analyzed data, Nemerow synthetic pollution index and multiple
   statistical methods were used for the contamination assessment, source
   identification and apportionment of anthropogenic heavy metals,
   respectively. The pollution indexes corresponding to Cu, Zn, Pb, Cr and.
   Cd were 3.31, 37.63, 4.97, 1.46 and 46.71, respectively. Meanwhile,
   metals like Cu, Zn, Pb and Cd were attributed to the severely polluted
   domain as a whole, whereas Cr alone belonged to lightly polluted. The
   Nemerow synthetic pollution index values. corresponding to different
   land-use types were in the following order of urban square \& park area>
   commercial area>educational area> industrial area>residential
   area>traffic area. The contributions of different sources to
   anthropogenic heavy metals were quantitatively determined by absolute
   principal component score-multiple linear regression (APCS-MLR):
   domestic and industrial wastes was responsible for 38.82\% of the Cu
   contamination, automotive vehicle 72.59\% of the Zn, building decoration
   and automotive vehicle 42.07\% and 49.33\% of the Pb respectively,
   plating products 73.98\% of the Cr and building decoration activities
   79.15\% of the Cd.},
