Title = {Emotiv Cities: Understanding Human Perception of Urban Spaces-A Case
   from South India},
Booktitle = {RECENT ADVANCES IN ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE FROM THE EURO-MEDITERRANEAN AND
   SURROUNDING REGIONS, EMCEI-4 2022},
Series = {Advances in Science Technology and Innovation},
Year = {2024},
Pages = {1103-1105},
Note = {4th Euro-Mediterranean Conference for Environmental Integration-EMCEI,
   Sousse, TUNISIA, NOV 01-04, 2022},
Abstract = {Neurourbanism is a less explored link, bridging the two streams of urban
   built environment studies and neuroscience, which are diverse yet
   connected in a cause-effect relationship. The advancements in
   neuroscience expose new ways to understand how individuals interact with
   the built environment, revealing new chances for innovation and improved
   experiences, leading to increased productivity, well-being and a better
   quality of life. This paper is an enquiry that explores human
   experiences in Indian urban spaces by drawing information from
   phenomenology, evidence-based design and academic literature. As a first
   step towards developing a neuroscience-based urban public space
   evaluation system in the light of understanding how humans experience
   urban spaces, this paper is a preliminary study that attempts to
   decipher how distinct urban settings influence human behaviour. This
   pilot study involved a small number of human EEG readings that were
   measured and recorded as the participants, equipped with a wireless
   5-channel EEG measuring device, traversed through urban spaces in
   different urban contexts. These spaces were a combination of various
   degrees of street enclosures with or without natural features in the
   city of Calicut in the South Indian state of Kerala. These EEG readings
   are analysed explicitly into bandwidths of various frequencies like
   alpha, beta, delta and theta and classified based on various performance
   metrics interpreting the emotional states the participants experience,
   namely excitement, engagement, interest, relaxation and stress. It was
   observed that the values of these performance matrices changed
   significantly as the environment changed from busy to calm, narrow to
   wide, urban to green and crowded to less occupied. The outcome of this
   research can boost the design disciplines in formulating their
   scientific methods for exploring the behaviour of people in the physical
   environment and understanding their perception of urban places.},
